messages:
elaine park: heads-up — onboarding-embeds sampleapp became throughput-starved after we added a 150ms per-request client timeout yesterday

ravi shah: metrics? are we seeing 429s or internal errors?

elaine park: weirdly no 429s. dashboard shows inflight hovering at the client window (16) and new prefetches get blocked. no server errors, just a lot of aborted requests

samir patel: do aborted/timeouts release the concurrency slot? iirc we had a bug where AbortError didn't decrement the semaphore in the JS SDK

elaine park: logs (sanitized):
```
[2026-04-10T12:00:01Z] prefetch:start id=cur-7 inflight=15
[2026-04-10T12:00:01Z] http GET /v1/embeds status=timeout id=cur-7
[2026-04-10T12:00:01Z] prefetcher: abort id=cur-7 reason=client_timeout
[2026-04-10T12:00:02Z] prefetch:start id=cur-8 inflight=16 limit=16 action=blocked
```

ravi shah: ugh. so aborted requests were never releasing the slot and we eventually deadlocked under steady churn

elaine park: repro script I used (JS):
```
// repro-abort.js
async function doOne(client){
  const ctr = new AbortController();
  setTimeout(()=>ctr.abort(), 150);
  try{
    await client.embeds.create({input: 'x'.repeat(64)}, { signal: ctr.signal });
  } catch (e) {
    // we see AbortError here
  }
}

// spawn 20 concurrent prefetchers that time out often
Promise.all(new Array(20).fill(0).map(()=>doOne(client)));
```

samir patel: confirmed locally. JS SDK only decrements inflight in the success and http-error branches, but AbortError flows out of the call earlier. Fix is to release the slot in finally (or attach an on-abort handler). i'll open a minimal PR and add a unit test simulating AbortController

elaine park: temporary mitigation — lowered the sampleapp prefetch window from 16 -> 8 and restarted pods to clear leaked slots. throughput returned to normal

samir patel: long-term: 1) ensure SDK always releases slot in finally for aborts/timeouts, 2) add a client opt-in `release_on_abort` that enforces strict slot release semantics (helpful for sensitive prefetch patterns), 3) add a parity test across go/python/js so other SDKs don't regress

ci-bot: canary deploy requested: sampleapp-staging release_on_abort=true by @samir patel

elaine park: thanks — i'll update onboarding docs with a note: "If you see steady blocked prefetches after adding client timeouts, restart clients or reduce prefetch window until SDK patch is applied."

samir patel: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/634 small fix + tests. follow-up: add CI parity job that simulates AbortController + windowed prefetch

ci-bot: pipeline: unit tests -> passed :white_check_mark:

ravi shah: nice find. i'll include this scenario in the sampleapp stress harness so teams can catch it early :thumbsup:
